<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">African Lakes Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The African Lakes Company was a Scottish trading company founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> in 1878 to support the Presbyterian missions around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a> and to replace the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> with legitimate commerce. From its base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karonga">Karonga</a> it fought Swahili-Arab slave traders in the Karonga War of 1887 to 1889 in Ngonde country, helping prepare the way for the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The company grew out of the Livingstonia Central Africa Company, set up by a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> businessmen: James Cochran Stevenson, John Stephen, James S Napier, James White and James Young, joined soon after by Alexander Low Bruce, the son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a>. All the original directors were connected with the Foreign Missions Committee of the Free Church of Scotland, the parent body of the Livingstonia Mission. It took the name African Lakes Company in 1878, when it meant to extend its work to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>, and its first managers were the brothers John Moir and Frederick Moir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>With a share capital of only £120,000 and poor trading results, the company was almost bankrupt by 1884. It traded mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, much of it supplied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karonga">Karonga</a> by Mlozi, who also dealt in slaves. Relations broke down as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> traders turned more to slaving and attacked Ngonde communities the company had promised to protect. The company also engaged Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>, who attacked the traders' stockades in 1888 and 1889 without success, and the failure ended its political claims in the area.</p><p>Between 1884 and 1886 the company made, or claimed to have made, treaties with chiefs around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karonga">Karonga</a>, and in 1886 it asked the British government for a charter. Missionaries opposed it and the government judged it unable to govern the region. After the British government proclaimed the Nyasaland Districts Protectorate in 1891, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> took control of it, and its tie to the Free Church ended. An inquiry of 1929 found its claim to over 2.7 million acres spurious: some treaties had never been made, and others were with men who were not chiefs of the land claimed.</p><p>The company ran steamers on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Nyasa</a>, the Shire River and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> from the port of Chinde, recruited migrant labour, employing 5,500 workers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shire-highlands">Shire Highlands</a> by 1894, and opened around 50 village stores known as Mandala stores by 1911.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Its successor, the African Lakes Corporation, turned to motor dealing and later to internet services in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, declined in the 1990s and was liquidated after selling its last asset in 2007. Its first base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blantyre">Blantyre</a>, Mandala House, is a National Monument and the oldest building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>, and the company's records are kept by Glasgow University Archive Services.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “African Lakes Corporation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scottish missions at Blantyre and Livingstonia and the <b>African Lakes Company</b> drew Britain into the Lake Malawi region.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-central-africa-protectorate">British Central Africa Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1880s the Swahili-Arab trader Mlozi set up fortified bases near Karonga, and the Ngonde were caught in the Karonga war of 1887–89 between Mlozi and the <b>African Lakes Company</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ngonde-kingdom">Ngonde Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
